  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "The binomial PMF sums to 1, has mean n*p and variance n*p*(1-p), has the expected probability generating function, is closed under convolution, and converges to the Poisson PMF under p = r/n.",
    "plain": "Backfills metadata for deriving binomial-distribution facts from the binomial theorem. The proof defines the binomial PMF, proves normalization, symmetry, mean, variance, PGF, convolution, and a fixed-k Poisson limit theorem.",
    "whyMatters": [
      "The binomial distribution is a core meeting point of algebra, combinatorics, and probability.",
      "The formalization shows how standard distribution facts flow from binomial identities and Vandermonde convolution.",
      "Including the Poisson-limit theorem connects the finite binomial model with a central approximation theorem."
    ]
  },
  "knownResults": {
    "proven": [
      "`binomPMF_sum_eq_one` proves normalization of the binomial PMF.",
      "`binomial_mean`, `binomial_second_factorial_moment`, and `binomial_variance` prove the first two moments.",
      "`binomial_pgf` records the probability generating function.",
      "`binomPMF_convolution` proves closure under convolution via Vandermonde's identity.",
      "`poisson_limit` proves fixed-k convergence of Bin(n, r/n) to the Poisson PMF.",
      "The exact `BinomialTheoremOQ03.lean` file is recorded with 0 axioms and 0 sorries."
